// batch ah: Midway, Stalingrad, El Alamein, Guadalcanal, Singapore, Kursk
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "porta-aviões": "A warship with a flat deck, from which aircraft take off and land. By 1942 it had taken the battleship's place as the main piece of the fleets, and whoever found the enemy fleet first had a great advantage.",
  "bombardeiro de mergulho": "An aircraft that plunges almost vertically onto its target and releases the bomb at close range, which gives very good accuracy. The Americans used the SBD Dauntless; the Japanese, the D3A.",
  "avião torpedeiro": "An aircraft that flies low and slow to drop a torpedo into the water against a ship. It has to come close and in a straight line, so it is very vulnerable to fighters and antiaircraft fire.",
  "patrulha aérea de combate": "Fighters that stay in the air around the fleet, ready to intercept enemy aircraft. When they are pulled down low, the fleet is left without protection up high.",
  "caça": "A fast, agile aircraft built to fight other aircraft and escort bombers.",
  "cruzador": "A fast, well-armed warship, smaller than a battleship and larger than a destroyer.",
  "destróier": "A small, fast ship, used to escort fleets, hunt submarines and launch torpedoes.",
  "criptoanalista": "A specialist in deciphering secret messages without having the code's key.",
  "atol": "A low coral island, shaped like a ring, around a lagoon.",
  "tanque": "An armored combat vehicle on tracks, with a rotating gun. The T-34 was the most common Soviet tank; the Germans used the Panzers, the Tiger and the Panther.",
  "blindado": "A combat vehicle protected by steel plates: a tank or an armored car.",
  "panzer": "German word for 'armored'. A panzer division is a division of tanks, with motorized infantry and artillery.",
  "tiger": "A German heavy tank, very well armored and with an 88 mm gun. It was expensive and hard to build, so there were few.",
  "panther": "A German medium tank built to face the T-34. It made its debut at Kursk, in 1943.",
  "luftwaffe": "The air force of Nazi Germany.",
  "eixo": "The alliance between Germany, Italy and Japan in the Second World War, with smaller allies such as Romania and Hungary.",
  "stavka": "The supreme military command of the Soviet Union, which answered to Stalin.",
  "grupo de exércitos": "A command that brings together several armies in one region. In 1942 the Germans had Army Groups A, B and Don in the south of the Soviet front.",
  "ponte aérea": "Supplying a place by air, when there is no way by land. It takes many aircraft and a good runway, and is easy to interrupt.",
  "franco-atirador": "A soldier trained to hit distant targets, one at a time, from hiding. In Stalingrad, they were very useful among the ruins.",
  "destacamentos de bloqueio": "Soviet units posted behind the front line, with orders to stop anyone who fled without authorization. Some deserters were shot by them.",
  "campo minado": "Ground full of mines, buried bombs that explode when a soldier or a vehicle passes over them. It forces the attacker to stop, or to lose time clearing a path.",
  "defesa em profundidade": "Defending in several belts, one behind another, instead of a single thin line. The attacker spends strength on each belt and reaches the last ones tired.",
  "canhão antitanque": "A long-barreled gun, built to pierce the armor of tanks. Hidden and well positioned, it is very dangerous.",
  "contraofensiva": "A major offensive by the defender, after withstanding and wearing down the enemy attack.",
  "barragem de artilharia": "A rain of shells from many guns at once over an area, to crush the defenses before the infantry advances.",
  "afrika korps": "The German force sent to North Africa in 1941, under Rommel. In 1942, together with Italian troops, it formed the Panzer Army Africa.",
  "ultra": "The name the British gave to intercepted and decrypted German messages, such as those from the Enigma machine. They revealed plans, positions and the enemy's fuel situation.",
  "diversão": "A false attack or movement, made so that the enemy looks in the wrong place.",
  "desembarque anfíbio": "An attack in which troops go from ships straight onto the enemy beach.",
  "fuzileiros navais": "The American Marines: naval soldiers trained to land and fight on shore.",
  "tokyo express": "The American nickname for the Japanese destroyer convoys that ran at night, carrying troops to Guadalcanal. The Japanese called the service 'rat transportation'.",
  "força z": "A small British squadron, with the battleship Prince of Wales and the battlecruiser Repulse, sent to Singapore in 1941. Japanese aircraft sank it on 10 December.",
  "estreito de johor": "The narrow channel, about one to two kilometers wide, between the island of Singapore and the southern tip of the Malay peninsula."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {

  midway: {
    war: "The Pacific War (1941–1945)",
    hook: "A broken code, an admiral's hesitation and a few minutes of diving changed the war in the Pacific.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "On 7 December 1941, Japan attacked the American base at Pearl Harbor. In the following months, it took the Philippines, Malaya, Singapore and the Dutch islands of Southeast Asia. The American [[aircraft carriers|porta-aviões]], which were not in port on the day of the attack, were still alive, and that is what worried Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto, the head of the Japanese fleet, most.",
        "Two things increased his haste. On 18 April 1942, American bombers launched from an aircraft carrier bombed Tokyo, the so-called Doolittle Raid. Little damage, but a great fright: the 'untouchable' island had been reached. And in May, in the Battle of the Coral Sea, the two sides traded blows with aircraft alone, without the ships ever seeing each other. The Japanese came out of it with one carrier damaged and another without enough aircraft, and both stayed out of what was coming.",
        { h: "The road to Midway" },
        { timeline: [
          ["7 Dec. 1941", "Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor. The war between Japan and the United States begins."],
          ["18 Apr. 1942", "The Doolittle Raid bombs Tokyo. Yamamoto now wants to destroy the American carriers as soon as possible."],
          ["4–8 May 1942", "Battle of the Coral Sea. Two Japanese carriers are left out of Midway, and the American Yorktown comes out damaged."],
          ["3 June 1942", "The Japanese begin the attack on the Aleutian Islands, to the north. An American aircraft spots the Midway invasion force."],
          ["4 June 1942", "The decisive day: Nagumo's four carriers are attacked."]
        ]},
        "Yamamoto's plan was to take Midway atoll, 1,300 miles (about 2,100 km) northwest of Hawaii. He expected the American fleet to rush to retake it, and then he would destroy it. It was a complex plan: the fleet was divided into several groups, and Yamamoto's battleships stayed hundreds of miles behind the carriers, too far away to help them. An attack on the Aleutians, to the north, was meant to serve as a distraction.",
        "But the Americans knew about the plan. At Pearl Harbor, Commander Joseph Rochefort's group of cryptanalysts, Station HYPO, was already reading part of the Japanese naval code. To be sure that 'AF' was Midway, they sent word, over an easily intercepted channel, that Midway's water plant had broken down. Shortly afterward, the Japanese transmitted that 'AF' was short of water."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Chester Nimitz, commander of the Pacific Fleet" },
        "Nimitz had taken over the fleet right after Pearl Harbor, when it was at its worst moment. He made the risky decision to trust Rochefort's codebreakers and to put at stake the few carriers he had. He calculated that the three carriers, added to the base at Midway, balanced Yamamoto's four, because the American air groups were larger.",
        { h: "Frank Fletcher and Raymond Spruance" },
        "Fletcher commanded the Yorktown and the American force as a whole. Spruance commanded the Enterprise and the Hornet, in place of Admiral Halsey, who fell ill and could not go. He was a cruiser admiral, not an aviation one, and still he made the two most important decisions of the day: launching the aircraft from far off, without waiting for them to form up, and, at night, not chasing the Japanese fleet into a possible trap.",
        { h: "Isoroku Yamamoto" },
        "He was the author of the Pearl Harbor plan, and he knew that Japan could not win a long war against the United States. That is why he wanted a decisive battle, early. His plan had many pieces, and all of them had to work.",
        { h: "Chuichi Nagumo and Tamon Yamaguchi" },
        "Nagumo commanded the carrier force, the same one as at Pearl Harbor. That morning, he was caught between Yamamoto's orders, his pilots' requests and news that arrived late. Rear Admiral Yamaguchi, head of the Hiryu's division, wanted to launch an immediate attack against the Americans, with whatever he had. Nagumo preferred to wait. Yamaguchi died with his ship the following day."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Japanese carrier force had four carriers (Akagi, Kaga, Soryu and Hiryu), with 248 aircraft, plus two battleships, cruisers and destroyers. The Americans had three carriers (Enterprise, Hornet and Yorktown), about 230 carrier-based aircraft, and more than 120 aircraft based on Midway, among fighters, bombers and seaplanes.",
        { note: "The number of aircraft changes from source to source. We use those of English Wikipedia, which gives 233 carrier-based aircraft and 127 land-based ones for the Americans. On 4 June, the Midway base had 122." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "None. It was open sea, north and northwest of Midway atoll, which has two flat islets, Sand and Eastern, with a landing strip. Whoever found the enemy first got the chance to strike first, and that is why search aircraft and information counted as much as weapons.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, on the morning of 3 June, an American seaplane spotted the Japanese invasion force, west of Midway. At dawn on 4 June, at 4:30 a.m., Nagumo launched 108 aircraft against the island and sent seven more to look for ships. At 5:34 a.m., an American seaplane spotted two Japanese carriers. Midway's radar detected the attack at 93 nautical miles (about 170 km).",
        "Second, the Marine fighters on Midway took off to intercept and were badly mauled: within minutes, only two were fit to fly. The base was bombed and heavily damaged. The leader of the Japanese strike told Nagumo that, if he wanted to land troops by 7 June, a second strike would be needed.",
        "Third, aircraft from Midway, which included six Avenger torpedo bombers, four B-26 bombers, dive bombers and B-17s, attacked the Japanese carriers in an uncoordinated way. They hit nothing, and many were shot down, but they kept the Japanese fighters busy. At 7:15 a.m., Nagumo ordered the torpedoes on the aircraft he was holding in reserve swapped for bombs to attack the island. At 7:40 a.m., a search aircraft reported American ships to the east. Nagumo ordered the swap reversed, which left the deck full of aircraft, bombs and fuel. At 8:20 a.m., news came that there was a carrier among them.",
        "Fourth, the Americans were already on their way. Spruance ordered the aircraft of the Enterprise and the Hornet launched just after 7 a.m., from a distance he considered extreme, without waiting for them to assemble. Those from the Hornet took the wrong heading, and the fighters escorting them ran out of fuel. The Hornet's torpedo squadron, VT-8, under Lieutenant Commander John Waldron, separated and found the Japanese alone, without fighters. At about 9:20 a.m., all 15 aircraft were shot down, without a single torpedo hitting. Only Ensign George Gay survived, of the 30 crew. Then came the torpedo bombers from the Enterprise (which lost 9 of 14) and the Yorktown (10 of 12), all without result.",
        "Fifth, and decisive. The torpedo bombers died, but the Japanese [[combat air patrol|patrulha aérea de combate]] dropped down to sea level after them. At that moment, the [[dive bombers|bombardeiro de mergulho]] from the Enterprise, under Commander Wade McClusky, arrived from above. He had kept searching when the Japanese fleet was not where expected and found it on seeing the destroyer Arashi. At 10:22 a.m., the two Enterprise squadrons went, through a misunderstanding, for the Kaga, while Lieutenant Richard Best, with a few aircraft, dived on the Akagi. The Soryu was hit by the Yorktown's bombers. The decks were full of armed aircraft and fuel, and within minutes three carriers were in flames. Nagumo moved to the cruiser Nagara at 10:46 a.m.",
        "Sixth, the Hiryu, which was farther off and escaped, launched two attacks against the Yorktown. The first, with 18 dive bombers and 6 fighters, scored three bomb hits. The second, with 10 torpedo bombers and 6 fighters, scored two torpedo hits. The Yorktown was badly damaged.",
        "Seventh, in the late afternoon, the Enterprise launched 24 dive bombers. Four or five bombs hit the Hiryu, which was abandoned and sank the following morning, with its captain and Rear Admiral Yamaguchi aboard.",
        "Eighth, Spruance, at nightfall, took operational command and withdrew to the east, partly because of a vague report from a submarine about 'four large ships'. Yamamoto cancelled the invasion. On 6 June, American aircraft sank the cruiser Mikuma, which had collided with the Mogami during the retreat. The same day, the Japanese submarine I-168 torpedoed the Yorktown and the destroyer Hammann. The Hammann sank, and the Yorktown rolled over and sank at 7:01 a.m. on 7 June."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Japan lost all four carriers (Akagi, Kaga, Soryu and Hiryu), the cruiser Mikuma and 248 aircraft. 3,057 Japanese died, 2,181 of them on the carriers. The Americans lost the Yorktown, the destroyer Hammann, about 150 aircraft and 307 men, among them Major General Clarence Tinker, who died in an aircraft accident.",
        "The number that weighed most was that of pilots: Parshall and Tully estimate that the Japanese lost 110 veteran aviators, about a quarter of those on the carriers. Japan lost four of the six large carriers it had in 1941, and could not replace them as quickly as the United States.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Midway ended the Japanese initiative in the Pacific. Two months later, the Americans landed on Guadalcanal, and it was Japan that began to react. For Japanese public opinion, the battle was a great victory: only the emperor and the navy's top brass learned of the true losses.",
        "It also showed the importance of information. The Americans won in large part because they knew where the Japanese would be and when, and because Yamamoto's plan, full of scattered pieces, depended on secrecy.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The wrecks: the Yorktown was found in 1998, at more than 5,000 meters' depth. Those of the Kaga and the Akagi were found in October 2019, at a similar depth." },
        { note: "The classic version: the story of the 'five minutes', in which the American aircraft arrived just as the Japanese were about to take off, comes largely from the account of Mitsuo Fuchida, who was aboard the Akagi. The historians Jonathan Parshall and Anthony Tully, in 'Shattered Sword', used Japanese sources to challenge it, and the debate over Nagumo's choices continues." },
        { note: "The name of the Guadalcanal airfield, Henderson Field, honors Major Lofton Henderson, commander of the Marine bomber squadron killed at Midway." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  stalingrado: {
    war: "Eastern Front of the Second World War (1941–1945)",
    hook: "A city named after Stalin became the trap that caught an entire German army.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union and came close to Moscow, but did not take it. In 1942, Hitler preferred a blow farther south. The summer offensive, Operation Blue, wanted the oil fields of the Caucasus and the Volga river, along which oil and much else went up to the north of the Soviet Union. Hitler split the army group in two: Group A went to the Caucasus, and Group B, with Friedrich Paulus's 6th Army, went to the Volga.",
        "Stalingrad was a long, narrow industrial city on the west bank of the Volga, with tractor and steel factories. It had had that name since 1925, in honor of Stalin, and that made it, in the eyes of both dictators, a political prize far greater than its military value.",
        { h: "The road to Stalingrad" },
        { timeline: [
          ["17 July 1942", "First clash between German and Soviet troops on the approaches to the city."],
          ["27 July 1942", "Stalin's Order 227 threatens with a tribunal anyone who retreats without orders. This is the 'Not one step back'."],
          ["23 Aug. 1942", "The Luftwaffe bombs the city, and the 6th Army attacks."],
          ["Mid-Sept. 1942", "General Chuikov takes over the 62nd Army, which defends the city."],
          ["19 Nov. 1942", "Operation Uranus falls on the Romanian flanks."],
          ["23 Nov. 1942", "The encirclement closes around the 6th Army."],
          ["2 Feb. 1943", "The last Germans in Stalingrad surrender."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Friedrich Paulus, commander of the 6th Army" },
        "A staff officer, better known for planning than for commanding troops. He obeyed Hitler to the end. On 30 January 1943, Hitler promoted him to field marshal, which many read as an invitation to suicide, since no German field marshal had ever surrendered. Paulus surrendered the next day.",
        { h: "Vasily Chuikov, commander of the 62nd Army" },
        "He took over the defense of the city in September 1942. He said the city would be defended or he would die trying. His tactic was to 'hug' the enemy: keeping the lines so close to the German ones that the Luftwaffe's artillery and aircraft could not fire without hitting their own soldiers.",
        { h: "Zhukov and Vasilevsky" },
        "The two prepared, for the [[Stavka|stavka]], the counteroffensive that encircled the Germans. Zhukov even postponed the start by two days because the air force was not ready. On 17 November, Vasilevsky wanted to cancel the operation because of a request for postponement from a corps commander. The other commanders overruled him.",
        { h: "Hitler and Manstein" },
        "Hitler forbade a breakout, and the pocket stayed trapped. Field Marshal Erich von Manstein, commanding Army Group Don, tried to save the 6th Army in December, and failed."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary. At the start of the offensive, the Germans and their allies had, according to the sources, 187,000 to 270,000 men, about 2,200 to 3,000 guns and 400 to 500 tanks. In the counteroffensive, the Soviets gathered between 1 million and 1.14 million men, 640 to 894 tanks and more than 13,000 guns. Operation Uranus alone used about 1.1 million men, 804 tanks and 13,400 guns, according to Wikipedia.",
        "To the north and south of the 6th Army, the line was held by Romanian armies, not by Germans. The Romanian 3rd Army covered about 140 km, and the 4th, about 270 km. They had few antitank guns, and the 37 mm ones were old and out of ammunition. Germany sent only six 75 mm guns per division.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The city stretched for dozens of kilometers along the west bank of the Volga, with the hill of Mamayev Kurgan in the middle, big factories and a grain elevator. Behind the Soviets lay the river, without bridges: reinforcements and ammunition crossed by boat, under fire. All around, the open steppe, where an armored thrust could run for hundreds of kilometers.",
        { h: "The battle in nine moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the fight in the city. On 23 August, the Luftwaffe bombed it and reduced a good part of it to ruins. The Germans went in, and the battle became street fighting: house by house, floor by floor, what German soldiers called 'rat war'. Pavlov's House, an apartment building, was defended by a platoon for about two months. In the grain elevator, about fifty men held out for five days. The sniper Vasily Zaitsev was credited with 225 kills. By mid-November, the Germans had pushed the Soviets into narrow strips along the river.",
        "Second, on 19 November, at 7:20 a.m., Operation Uranus began. From the north, the 5th Tank Army, with the 21st and 65th Armies, attacked the Romanian 3rd Army, which could not hold.",
        "Third, on 20 November, the Stalingrad Front attacked from the south, with the 51st and 57th Armies, against the flank defended by the Romanians. The two attacks raced toward Kalach, west of the city, where there was a bridge over the Don.",
        "Fourth, on 22 November, the Soviets crossed the Don at Kalach, and on 23 November, near Sovetsky, at 4 p.m., the two groups met. The 6th Army and part of the 4th Panzer Army were surrounded in a [[pocket|bolsão]]: 250,000 to 300,000 men, with about 100 tanks, 2,000 guns and mortars and 10,000 trucks. It was a [[double envelopment|cerco duplo]].",
        "Fifth, Hitler forbade the breakout. Between 23 and 24 November, he ordered an all-around defense, 'Fortress Stalingrad', and promised to supply the pocket by air. Between 680 and 750 tons a day were needed. The [[airlift|ponte aérea]] delivered, on average, 105. At the start of December, the 6th Army was receiving less than a fifth of what it needed.",
        "Sixth, in December, Manstein tried to break the encirclement from outside, with Operation Winter Storm, and on 18 December came within 48 km of the pocket. Paulus did not go to meet him: he had little fuel, and Hitler's order was to stay.",
        "Seventh, on 16 December, Operation Little Saturn attacked another sector of the front, farther west, and forced the Germans to abandon the relief attempt on 23 December. The next day, the Soviets took the Tatsinskaya airfield, from which the transport aircraft flew.",
        "Eighth, on 10 January 1943, Operation Ring began, with about 7,000 guns and mortars firing. The Pitomnik airfield fell on 16 January, and Gumrak's on the night of 21 to 22. Later, the pocket was cut in two.",
        "Ninth, on 31 January, the southern pocket fell, with Paulus. The northern one, commanded by General Strecker, surrendered on 2 February. Almost 91,000 men, among them 22 generals, were captured."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The 6th Army ceased to exist. It was the first time a Hitler field army had surrendered. The German government only admitted the defeat at the end of January, and Goebbels, on 18 February, gave the 'total war' speech at the Berlin Sportpalast.",
        "The losses are much debated. Axis casualties (killed, wounded and prisoners) lie between 800,000 and 1.5 million, depending on the sources, among Germans, Italians, Romanians, Hungarians and Russian volunteers. Soviet ones are 1,129,619 in the archives (478,741 killed or missing) and 1,347,214 in revised figures. In all, about 235,000 Germans and allies were captured during the whole battle. Of the roughly 91,000 at the end, 35,000 were sent off in transports and 17,000 of them did not survive. Only 5,000 to 6,000 returned home, in 1955.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Germany lost the initiative on the Eastern Front and never again managed a major summer offensive in the Soviet Union. It is the battle that most historians consider the turning point of the war in Europe. For the Soviets, confidence changed: Stalin was made a marshal, and the city received the title of Hero City in 1945.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Stalingrad numbers vary a great deal by source and by criterion: what counts as a 'casualty', where the battle begins and ends, who is included in the count. The ranges above come from the archives and from historians cited by Wikipedia, and none is definitive." },
        { note: "Order 227 and the [[blocking detachments|destacamentos de bloqueio]]: the detachments of the Stalingrad and Don fronts detained 51,758 men, and 980 were executed. In the 62nd Army, there were 203 arrests and 49 executions. The historian Antony Beevor spoke of 13,500 shot in total, a contested number. Historians also argue over whether what sustained the soldiers was coercion or patriotism." },
        { note: "Today the city is called Volgograd. At Mamayev Kurgan stands the monument 'The Motherland Calls', inaugurated in 1967. Every year the bodies of hundreds of soldiers are still found and buried." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  elalamein: {
    war: "Western Desert Campaign, Second World War (1940–1943)",
    hook: "Twelve days of attrition in a corridor of sand and mines, between the sea and nothing.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1942, the war in the desert of Libya and Egypt had already gone back and forth several times along the same road. Whoever had more supplies advanced, and whoever advanced too far ran out of fuel and fell back. In July, Erwin Rommel came to within about 100 km of Alexandria, threatening the Suez Canal and the oil of the Middle East. General Claude Auchinleck stopped him at the First Battle of El Alamein. On 30 August, at the battle of Alam el Halfa, Rommel tried again and did not get through.",
        "Churchill wanted a victory, and he wanted a new general. Auchinleck was replaced by Harold Alexander in command of the Middle East. General William Gott, chosen to command the 8th Army, died when his aircraft was shot down, and it was Bernard Montgomery who arrived, in August, in his place.",
        { h: "The road to El Alamein" },
        { timeline: [
          ["July 1942", "First Battle of El Alamein: Auchinleck stops Rommel's advance."],
          ["30 Aug. 1942", "Battle of Alam el Halfa: Rommel's last advance is contained."],
          ["23 Sept. 1942", "Rommel goes on sick leave. Georg Stumme takes over."],
          ["23 Oct. 1942", "At 9:40 p.m., 882 guns open the Second Battle of El Alamein."],
          ["4 Nov. 1942", "The British break the line. Rommel orders the retreat."],
          ["8 Nov. 1942", "Anglo-American landing in Morocco and Algeria (Operation Torch)."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Bernard Montgomery, commander of the 8th Army" },
        "He arrived in August 1942 and insisted on planning everything calmly. Before fighting, he gathered new tanks, better antitank guns and an air force that dominated the sky. He expected a 12-day battle, in three phases: opening, attritional fighting and the final blow. That is roughly what happened.",
        { h: "Erwin Rommel, the 'Desert Fox'" },
        "The most famous German general in the desert. He was sick and away from the field when the battle began: he returned on 25 October, a day after the death of his substitute, Georg Stumme, from a heart attack. He knew he had no fuel to maneuver, and planned a defense of minefields, in place of a mobile strike.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Alexander was Montgomery's superior, in command of the Middle East. Wilhelm von Thoma, who took over when Stumme died, commanded the Afrika Korps. Hitler, from afar, ordered Rommel not to yield a single yard of ground."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Allies had about 195,000 men, 1,029 tanks, 892 to 908 guns and 1,451 antitank guns, with 730 to 750 aircraft. The Axis had about 116,000 men, 547 tanks, 552 guns and 770 to 900 aircraft. The Allied advantage was also one of quality: Sherman tanks, 6-pounder antitank guns and Spitfire fighters.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The El Alamein line lay between the Mediterranean, to the north, and the Qattara Depression, of soft sand, which tanks could not cross, to the south, on a front of about 60 km. There was no going around: it had to be pierced. Rommel filled the front with [[minefields|campo minado]], about half a million mines, in belts and in the so-called 'devil's gardens'. And he split his armor into two groups, north and south, for lack of fuel to move it quickly.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the deception. In Operation Bertram, the British used dummy tanks and guns, false depots and a fake pipeline to suggest an attack farther south, two days after the real date. The real supplies were brought forward at night.",
        "Second, on 23 October, at 9:40 p.m., 882 field and medium guns fired about 529,000 rounds in five and a half hours. The infantry of XXX Corps advanced into the minefields to open two corridors. In the south, XIII Corps made [[diversionary|diversão]] attacks.",
        "Third, the tanks of X Corps were supposed to pass through the corridors, but got stuck in the dust and the traffic jams. The engineers could not clear the planned path. The Miteirya ridge was taken at 8 a.m. on 24 October, but no tank got through. In the late afternoon, more than 100 tanks clashed near Kidney, and half were destroyed before dark. Stumme died that day, and Rommel returned on the night of the 25th.",
        "Fourth, from 26 to 28 October, Allied aircraft sank the tankers carrying fuel to the Axis at Tobruk (Proserpina and Tergestea on the 26th, Luisiano on the 28th). On 27 October, at the Snipe post, the Rifle Brigade destroyed 22 German tanks and 10 Italian ones. On 28 October, Rommel's last major attempt to seize the initiative failed, and he warned that, without fuel, the army could not withdraw.",
        "Fifth, Operation Supercharge began in the early hours of 2 November, near Tel el Aqqaqir, after seven hours of air bombardment and a barrage from 360 guns, with 15,000 rounds. The 9th Armoured Brigade set out with 132 tanks and reached the start line with 94. Within half an hour, only 24 were fit to fight. Of the roughly 400 men in the attack, 230 were killed, wounded or captured. It was the blow that prepared the breach of the 4th.",
        "Sixth, on 2 November, Thoma warned that he would have at most 35 tanks the next day, and Rommel decided to fall back to Fuka. On 3 November, Hitler replied that the army should stay and 'not yield a single yard'. Rommel said the order demanded the impossible.",
        "Seventh, on 4 November, the British opened a gap about 19 km wide. At 5:30 p.m., Rommel ordered the retreat without waiting for Hitler's reply. For lack of trucks, the Italian infantry was largely left behind, and the Ariete, Littorio and Trieste divisions were destroyed.",
        "Eighth, the 8th Army pursued slowly and failed to cut Rommel off at Fuka or at Mersa Matruh. The 7th Armoured Division ended the 4th and 5th about 30 km from its objective. On 11 November, the New Zealanders took the Halfaya Pass and 600 Italian prisoners."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Allies lost 13,560 men: 2,350 killed, 8,950 wounded and 2,260 missing. Of these casualties, 58% were British, 22% Australian, 10% New Zealanders and 6% South African. They lost 332 to 500 tanks, but about 300 were repaired. Axis losses vary greatly among the sources: from 41,800 to 73,000 men, counting killed, wounded and prisoners, and about 500 tanks. On 4 November, 36 of the 249 German tanks at the start remained.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The threat to Egypt, the Suez Canal and the Middle East was over. A few days later, on 8 November, the Anglo-American landing in Morocco and Algeria opened a second front in North Africa. Rommel fell back across Libya, and the Axis forces in Africa surrendered in May 1943.",
        "It was the first major Allied success since Operation Crusader, in late 1941. For British morale, it mattered a great deal: Alexander suggested to Churchill that he have the church bells rung.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Churchill's phrases: on 10 November 1942, he said that this was perhaps 'the end of the beginning'. The line 'Before Alamein we never had a victory. After Alamein we never had a defeat' comes from his memoirs, published in 1950, and not from the heat of the moment." },
        { note: "Ultra: the British read Axis messages and knew the order of battle, the supply situation and Rommel's intentions. On 25 October, the Panzer Army had fuel for about three days." },
        { note: "The pursuit: Montgomery is criticized for having been slow after the victory, letting Rommel escape. Historians also debate why Rommel had gone so far: Martin van Creveld stresses the limits of supply, and Remy stresses the pressure from Hitler and Mussolini." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  guadalcanal: {
    war: "The Pacific War (1941–1945)",
    hook: "Over an airstrip in the jungle, Americans and Japanese fought for six months on land, at sea and in the air.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Midway, the Americans wanted to go on the offensive. The target was not large: Guadalcanal, an island of jungle and rain in the Solomon Islands, near where the Japanese were building an airstrip at Lunga Point. From there, Japanese aircraft would threaten the shipping routes between the United States and Australia.",
        "It was terrain that neither side knew well, and both entered the fight in a hurry. The Americans called the operation Watchtower. It was the Allies' first major land offensive against Japan, in an [[amphibious landing|desembarque anfíbio]].",
        { h: "The road to Guadalcanal" },
        { timeline: [
          ["July 1942", "The Japanese build the airstrip at Lunga Point. There were about 2,800 people on the island, among them 2,200 Korean laborers."],
          ["7 Aug. 1942", "About 11,000 Marines land on Guadalcanal and take Tulagi and the neighboring islands."],
          ["8–9 Aug. 1942", "Battle of Savo Island: Japanese cruisers sink four Allied cruisers."],
          ["21 Aug. 1942", "Battle of the Tenaru: Ichiki's regiment is destroyed."],
          ["12–14 Sept. 1942", "Battle of Edson's Ridge."],
          ["12–15 Nov. 1942", "Naval Battle of Guadalcanal."],
          ["1–7 Feb. 1943", "The Japanese evacuate the island. Operation Ke takes out 10,652 men."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Alexander Vandegrift" },
        "He commanded the 1st Marine Division, the landing force. He had little: about 11,000 men on the island at the start, after the ships left with part of the supplies. He held the area around the airstrip, which was named Henderson Field. He was replaced by General Alexander Patch on 9 December 1942.",
        { h: "Robert Ghormley and William Halsey" },
        "Ghormley commanded the South Pacific campaign until 18 October 1942, when Halsey replaced him.",
        { h: "Harukichi Hyakutake and Isoroku Yamamoto" },
        "Hyakutake commanded the 17th Army, which tried to retake the island in successive attacks, all repelled. Yamamoto commanded the fleet, which tried several times to destroy the American one and ended up losing many ships.",
        { h: "The two sides on the island" },
        "The [[Marines|fuzileiros navais]] defended the airstrip. On the other side, the Japanese soldiers arrived at night, in fast ships, and the food and ammunition they brought were never enough."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "In total, the Japanese sent about 31,400 army soldiers and 4,800 from the navy. The Allies, mostly Americans, had about 11,000 men at the start and passed 50,000 in January 1943. The troops of both sides were not all on the island at the same time.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Dense jungle, heat, rain, malaria and rivers full of mud. The Lunga Point airstrip was the center of everything: whoever controlled it commanded the sky of the region. The ships, at sea around it, fought at night. The channel between Guadalcanal and the neighboring islands became so full of wrecks that the Americans call it 'Ironbottom Sound'.",
        { h: "The campaign in six moments" },
        "First, on 7 August, the Marines landed by surprise. Tulagi fell on 8 August and the islets Gavutu and Tanambogo, by the 9th. On Guadalcanal, the airstrip was taken at 4 p.m. on the 8th, and by 18 August it was ready to operate. On 12 August, it was named Henderson Field.",
        "Second, on the night of 8 to 9 August, at the battle of Savo Island, Admiral Gunichi Mikawa's cruisers surprised the Allied fleet and sank one Australian cruiser and three American ones. Admiral Turner withdrew the Allied ships, leaving the Marines with fewer supplies and less equipment than they should have had.",
        "Third, the Japanese tried to retake the airstrip piecemeal. On 21 August, at the Tenaru river (Alligator Creek), the first group of Ichiki's regiment, with 917 men, attacked and was destroyed: 789 died. On 12 to 14 September, General Kawaguchi attacked the ridge that gives access to the airstrip and lost about 850 men, against 104 of the Marines.",
        "Fourth, the fight in the air and at sea. On the night of 14 October, two Japanese battleships fired 973 shells of 14 inches at the airstrip and destroyed 48 of the 90 aircraft of the Cactus Air Force, the air force that defended the base. The soldiers called it 'The Night'. On 23 to 26 October, Maruyama's 2nd Division attacked from the south and lost between 2,200 and 3,000 men, against about 80 American dead. On 26 October, at the battle of Santa Cruz, the carrier Hornet was sunk.",
        "Fifth, on 12 to 15 November, the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal. Rear Admirals Callaghan and Scott, Americans, died. The battleship Hiei was sunk after air attacks, and the Kirishima was heavily damaged by the American battleship Washington. The Japanese transports were beached and destroyed, and only 2,000 to 3,000 soldiers reached shore. On 30 November, at Tassafaronga, Japanese torpedoes sank one American cruiser and damaged three.",
        "Sixth, the Japanese gave way. The Navy proposed abandoning the island on 12 December, the imperial command agreed on the 26th, and the emperor approved on 31 December. By then, fewer than 15,000 Japanese remained on the island, growing weaker. In Operation Ke, from 1 to 7 February 1943, destroyers took off 10,652 men. Patch declared the island secure on 9 February."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Allies lost about 7,100 men, 29 ships (among them 2 carriers, 6 heavy cruisers, 2 light cruisers and 17 destroyers) and 615 aircraft. Japan lost about 19,200 army soldiers, about 8,500 of them in combat, and the rest to hunger and disease, plus 38 ships and 683 aircraft. There were seven major naval battles around the island.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The campaign marked the Allies' shift from defense to attack and gave them the initiative in the Pacific. Japan lost ships and aircraft it could not replace as quickly, and also lost experienced pilots (about 150 veteran carrier pilots at Santa Cruz alone).",
        "It also showed the cost of the jungle: Japan lost more men to disease and hunger than in combat.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Troop numbers vary. Wikipedia lists about 60,000 for the Allied side and 36,200 for the Japanese. The total of Japanese dead also varies: there is talk of 19,200 for the army and, in another count, of about 30,000 including the navy, about three quarters of them from causes outside combat." },
        { note: "The Japanese night destroyer convoys became known to the Americans as the [[tokyo express|Tokyo Express]]. Heavy equipment, most of the food and ammunition could not be carried this way, which helps explain the hunger." },
        { note: "The airstrip was named Henderson Field on 12 August, in honor of Major Lofton Henderson, a Marine aviator killed at the battle of Midway." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  singapura: {
    war: "The Malayan and Singapore Campaign (1941–1942)",
    hook: "A fortress built to face ships fell to soldiers who arrived on foot and by bicycle.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Singapore was the main British military base in Asia, at the southern tip of the Malay peninsula. Between the 1920s and 1930s, the British built a large naval base there at Sembawang, an enormous dry dock, opened in 1938, and coastal guns, among them five 15-inch guns. Because of this, the city was nicknamed the 'Gibraltar of the East'.",
        "The plan had a weak point: it counted on a fleet that would come from Europe. The travel time was estimated at 42 days in 1919 and at 180 days in 1939. In 1940, with the war in Europe, the military chiefs said no fleet was available for the Far East. It was also believed the jungle was almost impossible to cross. An exercise by General Dobbie, in southern Malaya, showed that it was 'far from impassable'.",
        { h: "The road to Singapore" },
        { timeline: [
          ["8 Dec. 1941", "The Japanese land at Kota Bharu, in Malaya, and the campaign begins."],
          ["10 Dec. 1941", "[[Force Z|força z]], with the ships Prince of Wales and Repulse, is sunk by Japanese aircraft."],
          ["Dec. 1941–Jan. 1942", "The Japanese come down the peninsula, with infantry on bicycles and light tanks, bypassing the British lines several times."],
          ["31 Jan. 1942", "The British blow up the causeway linking the island to the mainland."],
          ["8 Feb. 1942", "At night, the Japanese cross the Johor Strait."],
          ["15 Feb. 1942", "Percival surrenders."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Tomoyuki Yamashita, commander of the 25th Army" },
        "He commanded the Malayan campaign and became famous as the 'Tiger of Malaya'. He reached Singapore with tired troops, with little ammunition and fewer men than the British, and he knew it. After the war, he said the attack was a bluff, and that he feared the British would discover his weakness in numbers and supplies.",
        { h: "Arthur Percival, British commander in Malaya" },
        "He led about 85,000 men, but many were new, poorly trained or poorly equipped. He expected the main attack in the northeast and spread his troops along the coast, which left each sector weak. When the orders from Wavell and Churchill arrived, they left him no room to negotiate.",
        { h: "Wavell, Churchill and Gordon Bennett" },
        "General Wavell was the Allied commander of the region. On 10 February, Churchill cabled Wavell that 'there must at this stage be no thought of saving the troops or sparing the population', and that 'commanders and senior officers should die with their troops'. The Australian general Gordon Bennett, commander of the 8th Division, left the island by boat after handing over command, and this became a controversy."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British and allies (British, Indian, Australian and local troops) had about 85,000 men in Singapore. The Japanese had between 30,000 and 36,000, depending on the source. The Japanese dominated the air: the last Allied aircraft left Singapore on 9 or 10 February.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An island of about 40 km by 20 km, separated from the mainland by the [[Johor Strait|estreito de johor]], one to two kilometers wide. In its middle stood the hill of Bukit Timah, with the food and fuel depots, and the reservoirs that supplied the city. In the south, the city, with about a million civilians, plus refugees from the mainland.",
        { note: "The idea that Singapore's guns only pointed out to sea is an exaggeration. Three of the 15-inch guns could traverse 360 degrees and were used against the Japanese in the north. The problem was ammunition: they were supplied mostly with armor-piercing shells, made for ships, and had few high-explosive shells, more useful against soldiers." },
        { h: "The battle in six moves" },
        "First, before the fighting. On 31 January, the British blew up the causeway over the strait, which delayed the Japanese by more than a week. Percival expected the attack in the northeast and spread his troops along the coast.",
        "Second, on 8 February, shortly before 8:30 p.m., the first waves of the Japanese 5th and 18th Divisions began crossing the strait, in the northwest, not the northeast. About 13,000 men, in 16 assault battalions, attacked the Australian 22nd Brigade, which had about 3,000. Each division had 150 boats. That first night, 13,000 men landed, and another 10,000 after dawn. Japanese artillery fired 88,000 shells in fifteen hours, starting at 11 p.m.",
        "Third, the Jurong Line collapsed when two Indian brigades withdrew, for lack of communication and clear orders. On 10 February, Churchill ordered a fight to the finish.",
        "Fourth, on 11 February, the Japanese took the Bukit Timah area, with the main food and fuel depots. Yamashita wrote to Percival asking him to give up 'this futile and desperate resistance'. The Australians of the 22nd Brigade were reduced to a few hundred.",
        "Fifth, on 12 and 13 February, the British fell back to a perimeter of about 45 km around the city. The Japanese took the reservoirs on 13 February, but did not cut off the water. The problem was that a lot of water was lost through broken pipes, and the population was concentrated on about 1% of the island. On 14 February, Japanese soldiers killed staff and patients at Alexandra Hospital, and about 200 men were bayoneted after being held without water.",
        "Sixth, on 15 February, at 9:30 a.m., the British commanders concluded that a counterattack was impossible. Percival surrendered shortly after 5:15 p.m., at the Ford factory, where Yamashita would dictate the terms. Fire would cease at 8:30 p.m., and the British could keep 1,000 armed men to prevent looting."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "About 80,000 to 85,000 soldiers surrendered in Singapore, added to about 50,000 already captured in Malaya: in total, about 130,000 in the campaign. Allied combat casualties were about 5,000 killed or wounded, most of them Australians. Japanese ones were about 1,700 killed and between 2,800 and 3,400 wounded. Many prisoners died later, from mistreatment, disease and forced labor.",
        "Civilians suffered greatly: between 31,000 and 57,000 died during the battle and in the Sook Ching massacre that followed, which struck mainly the population of Chinese origin. Singapore was renamed Syonan-to and remained under Japanese occupation until the end of the war.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the largest surrender in British history, in Churchill's phrase, 'the worst disaster' in the country's military history. Together with the loss of Hong Kong and the sinking of the two big ships, it badly shook European prestige in Asia, and is seen as one of the factors that led to the end of British colonial rule in the region.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The blame: Wavell, in his report, blamed the Australians. Later historians point to desertions, the poor training of the reinforcements and Singapore's own strategy. Some also maintain that a counterattack could have worked." },
        { note: "The bluff: Yamashita said afterward that the attack was a bluff, that he was at the limit of his supplies and wanted to avoid house-to-house fighting. It is his version, told after the war." },
        { note: "The number of Japanese troops varies: Wikipedia gives a little over 30,000 in the text and 36,000 in the infobox. The numbers of civilians killed also vary widely, from 31,000 to 57,000." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  kursk: {
    war: "Eastern Front of the Second World War (1941–1945)",
    hook: "The Germans attacked a trap that the Soviets had spent months preparing.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Stalingrad, the Red Army advanced through the winter of 1942 to 1943. In February and March, Field Marshal Erich von Manstein answered with a counterattack, which retook Kharkov and Belgorod and left, on the front line, an enormous Soviet [[salient|saliente]] around the city of Kursk, which jutted like a wedge into the territory held by the Germans.",
        "The Germans saw a chance in it: attack from two sides, north and south, and cut it off at the base, trapping the Soviet troops inside. This was Operation Citadel. But Hitler postponed several times, waiting for the new [[Panther|panther]] tanks and for more [[Tiger|tiger]]s, and gave the Soviets time.",
        { h: "The road to Kursk" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Feb.–Mar. 1943", "Manstein's counterattack retakes Kharkov and Belgorod and leaves the Kursk salient."],
          ["15 Apr. 1943", "Hitler authorizes preparations for Operation Citadel."],
          ["6 May 1943", "The army high command sets the date for 12 June. Later, it postpones."],
          ["20 June 1943", "Hitler refuses to abandon the operation and fixes 5 July."],
          ["5 July 1943", "The German offensive begins."],
          ["12 July 1943", "Clash at Prokhorovka. Hitler orders Citadel stopped."],
          ["23 Aug. 1943", "The Soviets retake Kharkov."]
        ]},
        "The Soviets knew about the plan: British intelligence and the 'Lucy' spy ring informed them. Zhukov argued that the best response was to wait and wear down the German tanks in a prepared defense, before counterattacking. That is what they did."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Adolf Hitler" },
        "He approved the operation in April and postponed it until July. General Heinz Guderian, who looked after the tanks, was against the operation. On 12 July, at Görlitz, with Kluge and Manstein, Hitler ordered Citadel stopped, two days after the Allied landing in Sicily.",
        { h: "Erich von Manstein and Walter Model" },
        "Model commanded the 9th Army, which attacked from the north. Manstein commanded Army Group South, with Hermann Hoth's 4th Panzer Army and Detachment Kempf. Manstein criticized, in his memoirs, the decision to stop the offensive.",
        { h: "Georgy Zhukov and the Soviet commanders" },
        "Zhukov argued for the prepared defense. Rokossovsky commanded the Central Front, north of the salient, and Vatutin the Voronezh Front, to the south. Konev's Steppe Front stayed in reserve. General Pavel Rotmistrov commanded the 5th Guards Tank Army, which attacked at Prokhorovka."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Germans had, at the start, between 777,000 and 780,900 men, 2,450 to 2,930 tanks and self-propelled guns and 7,400 to 10,000 guns and mortars. The Soviets, inside the salient, had about 1.3 million men, 3,600 tanks, 20,000 artillery pieces and 2,792 aircraft, with more troops in reserve. Counting the reserves, some sources reach about 1.9 million men and 5,000 tanks.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Open, rolling steppe, cut by small rivers. The Soviets built a [[defense in depth|defesa em profundidade]]: three heavily fortified belts, with [[minefields|campo minado]], antitank ditches and [[antitank gun|canhão antitanque]] positions, and three more behind, less heavily manned. According to Wikipedia, the defense was about 300 km deep; other sources speak of eight belts over about 160 km. An analysis of Soviet documents counts at least 290,000 antitank mines and 294,000 antipersonnel mines.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the situation. The Germans were preparing a two-pronged attack, the 9th Army in the north and the 4th Panzer Army and Detachment Kempf in the south. The Soviets had two fronts on the combat line and one in reserve behind.",
        "Second, around 2 a.m. on 5 July, Zhukov sent an [[artillery barrage|barragem de artilharia]] against the German positions. It delayed some formations, but did not cause the losses he expected. The two German attacks began.",
        "Third, in the north, Model's attack stalled around Ponyri and Olkhovatka, from 7 to 10 July. The attack front, of 45 km, had shrunk to 15 km on 7 July. On the 8th and 9th, it advanced only 2 km. On 10 July, the Soviets had stopped him. Ponyri changed hands several times.",
        "Fourth, in the south, the attack went farther. The 2nd SS Panzer Corps broke through the first Soviet belt on 5 July and advanced about 29 km on 8 July. The 4th Panzer Army reached the Psel river on 9 July, and Hoth turned the corps northeast, toward Prokhorovka. On 8 July, Vatutin's counterattack, piecemeal and uncoordinated, failed.",
        "Fifth, on 12 July, at 8:30 a.m., Rotmistrov gave the order to attack with the words 'Steel, Steel, Steel'. About 500 Soviet tanks and self-propelled guns attacked in two waves (430 and 70) the 2nd SS Panzer Corps, which had 294 usable tanks and assault guns the night before. An antitank ditch about 4.6 m deep, at the foot of Hill 252.2, hampered the Soviet advance. Neither side reached its objective. By the end of the day, the Soviets were in the same positions.",
        "Sixth, on the same day, 12 July, the Soviets launched Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient, to the north, and Hitler ordered Citadel stopped. In the south, Operation Roland still dragged on from 14 to 17 July.",
        "Seventh, on 3 August, Operation Rumyantsev began, the Soviet offensive from Belgorod to Kharkov. Belgorod was liberated on 5 August.",
        "Eighth, on 23 August, the Soviets retook Kharkov, and the Soviets recovered territory along a front of about 2,000 km. It was the first time, in the war, that a German strategic offensive was stopped before breaking through the enemy lines, and the first in which a Soviet summer offensive succeeded."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Soviet losses, in the two phases (the defense and the counteroffensives), were about 863,000 men: about 254,000 killed, captured or missing and about 609,000 wounded or sick. In the defensive phase alone, Krivosheev's data give 177,877. German ones, according to medical data, were 165,000 to 203,000 men. In tanks and self-propelled guns, the Soviets lost 6,064 to 7,000 and the Germans at least 2,952 damaged or destroyed, of which 760 to 1,200 destroyed.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Red Army kept the strategic initiative until the end of the war and liberated Belgorod and Kharkov. It was also an industrial and intelligence victory: the Soviets knew, prepared and held.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Prokhorovka as 'the largest tank battle in history' is a myth, according to many historians. It came from Soviet accounts, above all Rotmistrov's. On 12 July, the Soviets lost about 340 tanks and self-propelled guns (destroyed or damaged), and the Germans 43 to 80, according to Wikipedia. The Russian historian Valeriy Zamulin arrives at a similar count (up to 80 German tanks against about 400 Soviet) and says the largest tank battle of the war was another one, a Soviet disaster. The German Karl-Heinz Frieser sees in Prokhorovka a German tactical victory, while Ben Wheatley maintains it was a Soviet victory, despite heavy losses. The numbers are much debated." },
        { note: "The Soviets had many losses. Rotmistrov said the 29th Tank Corps lost 60% of its armor and the 18th, 30%. Stalin was furious, sent Zhukov to Vatutin's headquarters, formed a commission, and even thought of a court-martial for Rotmistrov, until Vasilevsky intervened." },
        { note: "The Kursk numbers vary a great deal by source and by criterion: where the battle begins and ends, whether the two Soviet counteroffensives are counted, what counts as a 'lost' tank (destroyed or only damaged). That is why we give ranges." }
      ]}
    ]
  }

});

